<!-- FORECAST LOGIC -->

<section class="section">

<h2>Forecast-Based Storage Management</h2>

<p>
Each tank has its own rainfall inflow factor because the connected
roof areas differ. The forecast rainfall is therefore converted
into an expected water volume for every tank.
</p>

<div class="values">

<div class="value">
<strong>3.817 L/mm</strong>
Tank 1
</div>

<div class="value">
<strong>0.880 L/mm</strong>
Tank 2
</div>

<div class="value">
<strong>3.431 L/mm</strong>
Tank 3
</div>

<div class="value">
<strong>0.325 L/mm</strong>
Tank 4
</div>

</div>

<br>

<p>
Only if the current tank volume plus the expected rainfall exceeds
the available capacity does the system create additional storage space.
</p>

</section>



<!-- IMPLEMENTED AUTOMATION -->

<section class="section">

<h2>Automation Already Implemented</h2>

<div class="cards">

<div class="card">

<h3>Forecast-Based Transfer</h3>

<p>
Before expected rainfall, the system calculates whether storage
capacity is missing and automatically determines the required
transfer volume.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h3>Automatic CWH Supply</h3>

<p>
The CWH reservoir is automatically refilled when its defined
water level is reached.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h3>Overflow Protection</h3>

<p>
If a main tank reaches a critical level, water is automatically
transferred to another suitable tank.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h3>Adaptive Inflow Model</h3>

<p>
Measured rain events can be used to gradually improve the
tank-specific rainfall inflow factors.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</section>



